Body:
Repro / before-after summary:

Before
- custom `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` provider sends plain text or legacy `function_call` instead of modern `tool_calls`
- OpenCode sees a normal `stop` and the session ends after the first assistant reply

After with opt-in config
```json
{
  "provider": {
    "custom-provider": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "api": "https://api.example.com/v1",
      "options": {
        "toolParser": [
          { "type": "raw-function-call" },
          { "type": "json" },
          { "type": "single-tool-text", "tool": "bash", "argument": "command" }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
```
- outgoing `tools/tool_choice` can be rewritten into legacy `functions/function_call`
- incoming legacy `function_call` gets normalized into `tool_calls`
- text-only or tagged JSON tool intents can be recovered into a structured tool call before OpenCode consumes the response

That means broken custom providers can keep multi-turn sessions alive without changing behavior for normal providers, because the whole layer is opt-in through `provider.options.toolParser`.

Verified locally with:
- `bun test test/provider/openai-compatible-compat.test.ts`
- `bun test test/provider/copilot/copilot-chat-model.test.ts`
- `bun test test/session/llm.test.ts`
- `bun test test/provider/provider.test.ts`
- `bun run typecheck`

Heads up on the `headers` approach from @jacob-carlborg — it works, but there's a subtle header override issue worth noting.

OpenCode's anthropic provider has a hardcoded `CUSTOM_LOADER` that sets critical beta flags at the provider level ([provider.ts#L121-L130](https://github.com/sst/opencode/blob/dev/packages/opencode/src/provider/provider.ts#L121-L130)):

```typescript
async anthropic() {
  return {
    autoload: false,
    options: {
      headers: {
        "anthropic-beta":
          "claude-code-20250219,interleaved-thinking-2025-05-14,fine-grained-tool-streaming-2025-05-14",
      },
    },
  }
},
```

When you set model-level `headers` in config, `getSDK` merges them via object spread ([provider.ts#L1083-L1087](https://github.com/sst/opencode/blob/dev/packages/opencode/src/provider/provider.ts#L1083-L1087)):

```typescript
if (model.headers)
  options["headers"] = {
    ...options["headers"],    // provider-level (claude-code, interleaved-thinking, tool-streaming)
    ...model.headers,         // model-level (your context-1m config)
  }
```

Since both use the same key `anthropic-beta`, the model-level value **completely replaces** the provider-level one. So setting:

```json
"anthropic-beta": "context-1m-2025-08-07"
```

silently drops these three built-in beta features:

- `claude-code-20250219` — Claude Code specific capabilities
- `interleaved-thinking-2025-05-14` — extended thinking / chain-of-thought
- `fine-grained-tool-streaming-2025-05-14` — streaming tool call results

**Workaround**: manually include all flags as a comma-separated value:

```json
"headers": {
  "anthropic-beta": "claude-code-20250219,interleaved-thinking-2025-05-14,fine-grained-tool-streaming-2025-05-14,context-1m-2025-08-07"
}
```

This is fragile though — if OpenCode updates its built-in beta flags in a future release, the hardcoded value in your config becomes stale. Ideally the merge logic for `anthropic-beta` should concatenate rather than replace.

